{"id":80,"course_id":20,"title":"FinOps Review Cadence and Guardrails","summary":"Make cost optimization a durable operating habit instead of a panic exercise.","sequence":4,"body":"## Mental model\nFinOps works when teams have a regular review cadence, guardrails, budgets, anomaly detection, exception process, and a way to evaluate savings against reliability and delivery impact.\n\n## What to practice\nPractice writing cost guardrails: required tags, namespace quotas, idle resource reports, log retention tiers, load balancer review, budget alerts, and exception expiry.\n\n## Practice checkpoint\nBuild a monthly platform FinOps review agenda with business context, technical recommendations, risks, owners, and follow-up evidence.\n\n## Operator habit\nWrite down the symptom, the evidence, the suspected owner, and the safest next command before changing any live object. This turns scattered platform knowledge into a repeatable engineering workflow.","practice_notes":"Scenario: You are the platform engineer on call for a realistic team environment.\nChecklist:\n- State the user impact in one sentence.\n- Identify the system boundary and owner.\n- Collect read-only evidence first.\n- Propose the smallest reversible change.\n- Capture what should become a dashboard, alert, runbook, or automation.\nCommands:\n$ kubectl get events -A --sort-by=.lastTimestamp\n$ kubectl describe pod POD_NAME -n NAMESPACE\n$ kubectl logs deploy/APP -n NAMESPACE --since=15m","audio_url":null,"video_url":null,"course_slug":"platform-finops-kubernetes-aws","course_category":"FinOps","course_era":"Platform Academy","terms":[{"id":396,"term":"FinOps for Kubernetes and AWS Platforms","context":"FinOps","definition":"Learn cost visibility, ownership, right-sizing, Kubernetes waste, AWS EKS cost drivers, guardrails, and finance-friendly tradeoff communication."},{"id":397,"term":"Evidence","context":"Debugging","definition":"Observed facts from commands, dashboards, logs, traces, events, or cloud APIs."},{"id":398,"term":"Blast radius","context":"Reliability","definition":"The set of users, tenants, workloads, or systems affected by a failure or change."},{"id":399,"term":"Rollback","context":"Operations","definition":"A planned path to return to a known-good state when a change causes harm."},{"id":400,"term":"Runbook","context":"Operations","definition":"A step-by-step guide that helps responders diagnose and mitigate an incident safely."}],"flashcards":[{"id":238,"lesson_id":80,"prompt":"What is the main goal of FinOps Review Cadence and Guardrails?","answer":"Make cost optimization a durable operating habit instead of a panic exercise.","hint":"platform","difficulty":"beginner"},{"id":239,"lesson_id":80,"prompt":"Why collect evidence before changing live systems?","answer":"It reduces guessing, protects users, and makes the fix reviewable.","hint":"platform","difficulty":"beginner"},{"id":240,"lesson_id":80,"prompt":"What should a learner produce after each lab?","answer":"A clear diagnosis, a safe action plan, and a reusable note for future incidents.","hint":"platform","difficulty":"beginner"}]}
